arguments; precision mismatch: f64 vs f32

Nautilus.Special is generic over one `prec: Float` binder per function, so
every argument of a single call shares one float dtype. The first argument
binds `prec` to f64 and the second must then be f64 too.

The argument order matters, and is what makes this case discriminating. On the
f32-only module this same file is rejected the other way round -- `expected
f32, got f64`, because `beta`'s own declared f32 rejects the first argument.
Only a module whose first argument is free to bind f64 produces the text
pinned above, so this case cannot pass by accident against the pre-generic
module.
